,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":569.324719446,"data":"--- Problem tech_vieta_formulas [algebra.polynomials.vieta_formulas] ---\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":569.324720826,"data":"Triggers: polynomial coefficients and need symmetric expressions of roots, sum/product of roots without finding them, system of equations with symmetric variables as roots of auxiliary polynomial\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":569.324722463,"data":"Technique: Vieta formulas: for poly a_n*x^n+...+a_0 with roots r_i, e_k(roots) = (-1)^k * a_{n-k}/a_n. For quadratic x^2-Sx+P: r1+r2=S, r1*r2=P. For cubic x^3-Sx^2+Px-Q: sum=S, pairwise=P, product=Q. Use to build auxiliary polynomial from symmetric system.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":569.324724068,"data":"Question: If a+b+c=6, ab+bc+ca=11, abc=6, find a^2+b^2+c^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":569.324727936,"data":"Answer: a,b,c are roots of t^3-6t^2+11t-6=0 = (t-1)(t-2)(t-3). But we can also compute directly: a^2+b^2+c^2 = (a+b+c)^2 - 2(ab+bc+ca) = 36-22 = 14. This is the fundamental use of Vieta: express any symmetric function of roots in terms of elementary symmetric polynomials (which equal polynomial coefficients up to sign). Newton sums extend this to power sums recursively.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":569.32473001,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":569.324731096,"data":"--- Problem 06788e [algebra.sequences.linear_recurrence] ---\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":569.324732366,"data":"Triggers: a_{n+1}=f(n)-k*a_n, monotonicity constraint, closed form threshold\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":569.324733766,"data":"Technique: Linear recurrence a_{n+1}=2^n-7a_n with monotonicity constraint. Find minimal 1/a_0 such that a_{n+1}\u003ea_n for all positive n. The recurrence has closed form; the constraint becomes an inequality on a_0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":569.324736087,"data":"Question: A sequence of real numbers {a_n} satisfies that: a_{n+1}=2^n-7a_n, n=0,1,2,... Find the minimal possible value of 1/a_0 such that a_{n+1}\u003ea_n for any positive integer n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":569.324737673,"data":"Answer: Solve the linear recurrence a_{n+1}=2^n-7a_n. The homogeneous solution is C*(-7)^n. The particular solution involves 2^n terms. Express a_n in terms of a_0, then impose a_{n+1}\u003ea_n for all n\u003e=1. This gives a threshold for a_0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":569.324739348,"data":"\n"}
